{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And the later generation shall say, etc.</b> There are difficulties in this matter, for Scripture did not specify what the later generation and the foreigner who comes from a distant land shall say. For one cannot say that they will say: 'עַל מָה עָשָׂה ה׳ כָּכָה לָאָרֶץ הַזֹּאת' — for concerning this it says: ״וְאָמְרוּ כָּל הַגּוֹיִם עַל מָה עָשָׂה ה׳ כָּכָה״, and if so, what will your children say? And what is the meaning of saying first: ״הַנָּכְרִי״, and afterward saying: ״וְאָמְרוּ כָּל הַגּוֹיִם״? Moreover, perforce, before they see the plagues of the land they would say nothing, and if so why did he say ״וְאָמַר הַדּוֹר הָאַחֲרוֹן״ before ״וְרָאוּ אֶת מַכּוֹת הָאָרֶץ״?" "And the Rabbi Don Isaac Abrabanel explained that the first verse does not yet specify what they will say, but states simply: ״וְאָמַר הַדּוֹר הָאַחֲרוֹן בְּנֵיכֶם אֲשֶׁר יָקוּמוּ מֵאַחֲרֵיכֶם וְהַנָּכְרִי״ — for both of them will speak concerning this matter; and afterward he specified what all the nations will say concerning Israel: ״עַל מָה עָשָׂה ה׳ כָּכָה״, and your children will answer: ״עַל אֲשֶׁר עָזְבוּ״ etc. But his explanation does not suffice for all the difficulties we have raised, for first he called him ״נָכְרִי״, and afterward said ״כָּל הַגּוֹיִם״. In short, his explanation does not suffice at all to settle the verses in their proper order." "And I say that these verses speak of matters one at a time, for first he said: ״פֶּן יֵשׁ בָּכֶם אִישׁ אוֹ אִשָּׁה אוֹ מִשְׁפָּחָה אוֹ שֵׁבֶט״, and in this verse he speaks both of an individual who sins and of a public that sins. Then he went back and explained each in its own order — the first in its place and the last in its place. For from ״וְהָיָה בְּשָׁמְעוֹ אֶת דִּבְרֵי הָאָלָה הַזֹּאת״ until ״וְרָאוּ אֶת מַכּוֹת הָאָרֶץ״ — all of it speaks of the punishment of an individual who sins; and from ״וְרָאוּ אֶת מַכּוֹת הָאָרֶץ״ until the end of the parashah — all of it speaks of the sin of the public. \n\nNow you must know that such is the way of the Holy One, blessed be He: when an individual sins, he is stricken in his body according to his wickedness; but when the public sins, it is impossible to fulfill concerning them what is written concerning the individual: ״וּמָחָה ה׳ אֶת שְׁמוֹ מִתַּחַת הַשָּׁמָיִם״ — for to destroy the name of Israel is impossible, both because HaShem has already sworn to the Patriarchs not to exchange them for another nation, and because the twelve tribes of Israel correspond to the twelve constellations in the firmament, and with the nullification of even a single tribe the constellation in the firmament that leans upon it would fall. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, vents His wrath upon wood and stones and upon that land: ״גָּפְרִית וָמֶלַח שְׂרֵפָה כָּל אַרְצָהּ״. \n\nAnd justice demands that the land be stricken, for it was the cause of the first sin of the first man, Adam, by means of the matter it placed within him — as we have demonstrated with clear proofs above in parashat Acharei Mot (Leviticus 18:25) on the verse ״וַתִּטְמָא הָאָרֶץ וָאֶפְקֹד עֲוֹנָהּ עָלֶיהָ״ etc. Therefore in every sin of the public the land is stricken, as in the generation of the Flood, where it is written: ״וַתִּשָּׁחֵת הָאָרֶץ״. And likewise: ״וַתָּקִא הָאָרֶץ אֶת יוֹשְׁבֶיהָ״ — for it resembles a sick person who vomits and cannot bear the food that is proper for him; so too the land is stricken, for it was the cause of all sin and iniquity found in every generation. But in the case of an individual's sin, if the land were stricken the damage would reach even one who did not sin." "And this is what he said first concerning the punishment of a man or woman whose heart turns away from HaShem: ״כִּי אָז יֶעְשַׁן אַף ה׳ בָּאִישׁ הַהוּא״ etc., ״וְהִבְדִּילוֹ ה׳ לְרָעָה״ etc., ״וְאָמַר הַדּוֹר הָאַחֲרוֹן בְּנֵיכֶם״ etc., ״וְהַנָּכְרִי אֲשֶׁר יָבֹא מֵאֶרֶץ רְחוֹקָה״ — for both of them will speak and recount wondrous things, the plagues that HaShem sent upon that individual. And even though Scripture did not specify what they will say, nonetheless this is exactly like what is said in the reproof (Deuteronomy 28:37): ״וְהָיִיתָ לְמָשָׁל וְלִשְׁנִינָה״ — Rashi explained: ״וְלִשְׁנִינָה״ — a term of speech, they will speak about you, as its Targum renders: ״וּלְשׁוֹעִי״. And even there too, what they will say was not specified — rather, presumably they will speak about what they see in you from the greatness and wonder of the plagues. So too what is said here: ״וְאָמַר הַדּוֹר הָאַחֲרוֹן״ etc. ״וְהַנָּכְרִי״ etc. — both of them will express, as a wondrous novelty, what happened to that man. \n\nAnd specifically ״הַדּוֹר הָאַחֲרוֹן״ or ״הַנָּכְרִי אֲשֶׁר יָבֹא מֵאֶרֶץ רְחוֹקָה״ will say and speak to one another, as a wondrous novelty, what happened to this man; but that generation itself, and likewise the nearby nations who saw with their own eyes those plagues and know the man and his conduct — they will surely judge him guilty as a matter of certainty and will justify the judgment upon him. However, the later generation and the foreigner who did not know him will say to one another about what happened to him, and it will be to them a מָשָׁל וּלְשׁוֹעִי." "And afterward he went back to explain the last of the last, namely concerning what was said above: ״אוֹ מִשְׁפָּחָה אוֹ שֵׁבֶט״ — and they without doubt served idolatry openly, for if an individual blesses himself in his heart, then in the case of a public that has gathered for evil it is not applicable to say that they would serve in secret. And if so, if the whole congregation of Israel did not protest against them, it is just that all should be seized in the iniquity of that family or tribe. \n\nAnd concerning this he said: ״וְרָאוּ אֶת מַכּוֹת הָאָרֶץ״ — for when the public has turned from the way, to destroy them entirely is impossible; therefore HaShem will vent His wrath partly upon that land and partly upon them, for they will be exiled from it. And all the peoples will see ״אֶת מַכּוֹת הָאָרֶץ הַהִיא״ etc., ״גָּפְרִית וָמֶלַח שְׂרֵפָה כָל אַרְצָהּ״ etc., and concerning this all the nations will wonder, both near and far: ״עַל מָה עָשָׂה ה׳ כָּכָה לָאָרֶץ הַזֹּאת״ — that is to say, why was the land destroyed? It did not transgress and it did not sin. They raise the difficulty and they provide the answer: ״וְאָמְרוּ עַל אֲשֶׁר עָזְבוּ אֶת בְּרִית ה׳ אֱלֹהֵי אֲבוֹתָם״ — it resembles the sin of the first man, Adam, concerning whom our Sages of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 38a, and cf. Rabbenu Chananel there): he was a denier of the root principle. And the earth is what caused him to sin, by means of the coarse and turbid matter it placed in him beyond what HaShem had commanded it; and because the man was cursed for his iniquity, it too was punished for its iniquity and was cursed, as it says (Genesis 3:17): ״אֲרוּרָה הָאֲדָמָה בַּעֲבוּרֶךָ״. Therefore these too, who were deniers of the root principle, it is just that both should be punished — the land and those who serve idolatry. \n\nThis is what it says: ״וַיִּחַר אַף ה׳ בָּאָרֶץ הַהִיא״ etc., ״וַיִּתְּשֵׁם ה׳ מֵעַל אַדְמָתָם״ — to divide the punishment between them, for in this way He would not destroy the seed of Israel, since the punishment is split in two. ״וַיַּשְׁלִכֵם אֶל אֶרֶץ אַחֶרֶת״ — so that they would not be in that land from which their matter, which caused them to sin, is nurtured." "And the fact that a large lamed appears in the word ״וַיַּשְׁלִכֵם״ is a hint that after thirty generations they will be exiled from their land: for from Abraham to Solomon is fifteen generations, and from Solomon to Zedekiah is also fifteen generations, as is found in Rabboti (Exodus 15:26) on the verse ״הַחֹדֶשׁ הַזֶּה לָכֶם״ — that the kingship of Israel is likened to the moon, which stood for no more than thirty generations, like the days of the moon. Therefore a large lamed appears in ״וַיַּשְׁלִכֵם אֶל אֶרֶץ אַחֶרֶת״. And what is said: ״כַּיּוֹם הַזֶּה״ — this tells you: since they endure כַּיּוֹם הַזֶּה, therefore it is impossible to annihilate them utterly. Therefore the punishment was divided between them and the land." "And he said: <b>״הַנִּסְתָּרוֹת לַה׳ אֱלֹהֵינוּ״</b> and so forth — to resolve the change stated above regarding the sin of an individual man or woman: ״וְהִבְדִּילוֹ ה׳ לְרָעָה״ — and all Israel are clear; whereas regarding the sin of a family or tribe it is written: ״וְרָאוּ אֶת מַכּוֹת הָאָרֶץ״ and so forth — implying that all Israel are included in the punishment. To this he said: ״הַנִּסְתָּרוֹת לַה׳ אֱלֹהֵינוּ״ — for the individual who blesses himself in his heart, saying 'peace will be mine' — who knows what is in his heart but HaShem alone? Therefore ״וְהִבְדִּילוֹ ה׳ לְרָעָה״. But the sin of a family or tribe cannot take place in secret; therefore the obligation of purging idolatry from the land falls upon all Israel — ״וְהַנִּגְלוֹת לָנוּ וּלְבָנֵינוּ עַד עוֹלָם״ — and if they do not purge them from the land, then it is just that all of them be held liable for the iniquity of that tribe. This is what it says: ״וְהַנִּגְלוֹת לָנוּ וּלְבָנֵינוּ״ and so forth. This appears straightforward and correct when all the verses are settled in their proper place."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {5 "The citation 'רבתי (שמות טו כו) בפסוק החדש הזה לכם' is unusual: Shemot Rabbah 15:26 does not correspond to the parashah of 'החדש הזה לכם'. The reference may be to Shemot Rabbah 15 generally or to another section; rendered as found."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:15:23.942366248Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}